# Session 10

## 1. Objectives

* Install and run OpenBCI GUI on Windows 11.
* Connect OpenBCI Cyton to OpenBCI GUI through the USB dongle.
* Confirm that the Cyton live data source is available in the GUI.
* Start a manual serial connection through the detected COM port.
* Confirm that the board-to-GUI streaming path works.
* Check basic Hardware Settings for the initial EEG setup.
* Test the initial two-channel posterior headband montage:

  * Ch1 / N1P: posterior-left headband position
  * Ch2 / N2P: posterior-right headband position
  * SRB / SRB2 function: one earclip reference
  * BIAS: opposite earclip
* Determine whether Ch1 and Ch2 can show stable, non-railed, real-time scalp-contact waveforms.
* Avoid EEG feature interpretation until stable acquisition is confirmed.

## 2. Research question

Can OpenBCI GUI establish a stable Cyton data stream and display usable real-time waveforms from the initial headband-based EEG montage?

This session is not intended to interpret alpha, beta, attention, focus, or robot-control features.

The immediate purpose is to separate the following layers:

```text
GUI installation
→ Cyton / USB dongle connection
→ live data streaming
→ channel path functionality
→ electrode-skin contact stability
→ later EEG feature extraction
```

